1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,418
There was one company on Earth that Albert Wesker
could not buy, and it was the only one

2
00:00:13,438 --> 00:00:15,596
that frightened Catherine Adeyemi.

3
00:00:16,167 --> 00:00:22,432
Tianhe Genomics was headquartered in Shenzhen, in a
tower of green glass that looked across

4
00:00:22,452 --> 00:00:28,442
the water at Hong Kong. It had begun, twenty years
before, as a gene-sequencing company

5
00:00:28,462 --> 00:00:34,797
that sold its services cheaply to hospitals across
China, and it had grown, with the patient

6
00:00:34,817 --> 00:00:41,290
support of the Chinese state, into something that the
American intelligence services described

7
00:00:41,310 --> 00:00:47,783
in their classified reports as a national champion, and
that Tianhe's own employees described,

8
00:00:47,803 --> 00:00:50,408
on their first morning, as the future.

9
00:00:50,978 --> 00:00:57,383
It had sequenced the genomes of four hundred million
people. It had the largest biological

10
00:00:57,403 --> 00:01:03,592
database in human history. And it answered, in the
end, not to its shareholders, but to

11
00:01:03,612 --> 00:01:04,164
Beijing.

12
00:01:06,233 --> 00:01:10,629
It had also, since twenty thirty-one, been
trying to steal from Umbrella.

13
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:16,986
Not with rifles. With patience. Umbrella's Singapore
plant employed four thousand people.

14
00:01:17,006 --> 00:01:22,532
Nine of them, Hale's people had discovered over
three years, were reporting — through

15
00:01:22,552 --> 00:01:28,339
relatives, through friends, through a very old and
very careful network — to the Ministry

16
00:01:28,359 --> 00:01:33,951
of State Security in Beijing. Hale had not removed
them. The Director had told him not

17
00:01:33,971 --> 00:01:40,019
to. It was the same principle as Howard Lessing: a spy
you have found is a radio you control.

18
00:01:40,588 --> 00:01:46,798
But in the spring of twenty thirty-four, the radio
began to receive something the Director

19
00:01:46,818 --> 00:01:52,959
had not put there. Somebody in Beijing had begun
asking the nine to find out one specific

20
00:01:52,979 --> 00:01:57,182
thing. Not the formula for Aeterna. Not
the vaccine platform.

21
00:01:57,751 --> 00:02:00,966
They wanted to know where the
Director's blood was kept.

22
00:02:03,537 --> 00:02:06,704
Wen Shulan had been watching him
for three years.

23
00:02:07,274 --> 00:02:13,305
She had watched the eleven seconds, and the ghost
video, and the Court of Chancery, where

24
00:02:13,325 --> 00:02:19,289
he had taken off his sunglasses and shown a courtroom
a pair of perfectly ordinary eyes.

25
00:02:19,310 --> 00:02:25,069
She had read Owen Carrick's affidavit, and Exhibit
Twenty, and footnote fourteen. She

26
00:02:25,090 --> 00:02:30,849
had read the German patent analysis and the French
report on the twelve Russians. And

27
00:02:30,870 --> 00:02:36,765
she had read, from the Ministry's own sources in
Moscow, a copy of an order that an FSB

28
00:02:36,786 --> 00:02:42,614
colonel named Arkady Belov had given in January of
twenty thirty-one. Find out what he

29
00:02:42,634 --> 00:02:46,421
is. And when you have found out, tell
me how to kill it.

30
00:02:46,991 --> 00:02:52,450
Wen did not want to kill it. She was not a
sentimental woman, and she did not believe

31
00:02:52,470 --> 00:02:57,928
in revenge. She believed in advantage. And she had
concluded, after three years, that

32
00:02:57,948 --> 00:03:03,536
whatever made Albert Wesker what he was — whatever
let him move at three hundred metres

33
00:03:03,556 --> 00:03:09,143
a second, and choose the colour of his own eyes, and
file patents with no parents — was

34
00:03:09,163 --> 00:03:11,334
in him. In his body. In his blood.

35
00:03:12,024 --> 00:03:17,298
If we cannot buy the company, and we cannot copy the
company, then we take the source.

36
00:03:19,818 --> 00:03:25,733
The Director went to Hong Kong in May, for a
medical conference at which Umbrella was

37
00:03:25,753 --> 00:03:31,667
the principal sponsor. He knew what was waiting for
him. The nine radios in Singapore

38
00:03:31,687 --> 00:03:36,554
had told him, three weeks before, exactly
what Beijing had asked them.

39
00:03:37,245 --> 00:03:41,894
You are walking into their city. Into
their net. With two guards.

40
00:03:42,413 --> 00:03:47,530
I'm walking into a conference, with two guards and a
keynote. And I'd like to meet the

41
00:03:47,550 --> 00:03:52,607
woman who's been watching me for three years. She's
the only one of them who's clever

42
00:03:52,627 --> 00:03:54,100
enough to be interesting.

43
00:03:54,621 --> 00:03:56,692
And if she tries to take you?

44
00:03:57,211 --> 00:04:02,319
Then I'll learn something about Chinese
intelligence. And she'll learn something about

45
00:04:02,339 --> 00:04:02,497
me.

46
00:04:04,519 --> 00:04:06,323
They tried on the second night.

47
00:04:06,893 --> 00:04:13,424
It was very well done. The Director would say so
afterwards, with real professional admiration.

48
00:04:13,444 --> 00:04:19,562
A power failure in the hotel's east tower, at ten
past two in the morning — not complete,

49
00:04:19,582 --> 00:04:25,424
just enough to put the security cameras on backup
and the lifts on emergency power. A

50
00:04:25,444 --> 00:04:31,285
fire alarm on his floor, which sent the two Silent
Ones outside his door to check the

51
00:04:31,305 --> 00:04:37,561
stairwells, as their protocol required. And seven men
and women from the Ministry's special

52
00:04:37,581 --> 00:04:43,630
operations bureau, in the uniforms of hotel
maintenance staff and fire marshals, waiting

53
00:04:43,650 --> 00:04:49,630
for him in the service corridor behind his suite,
with a stretcher, and a sedative that

54
00:04:49,650 --> 00:04:53,629
could have put down a horse, and a van
in the loading bay.

55
00:04:54,199 --> 00:04:59,544
He walked into the corridor on purpose. He had
watched them take their positions, in the

56
00:04:59,564 --> 00:05:04,787
Thought Acceleration, from the other side of the
door. Three trains of thought, at six

57
00:05:04,807 --> 00:05:10,151
hundred to one. In one of them, he had already
counted their weapons. In another, he had

58
00:05:10,171 --> 00:05:15,333
worked out which of them was in charge. In the
third, he was thinking about something

59
00:05:15,353 --> 00:05:20,270
else entirely — about a woman in Beijing, and
what she would do when this failed.

60
00:05:21,841 --> 00:05:25,308
It took eleven seconds. He did
not kill anyone.

61
00:05:25,877 --> 00:05:32,187
That was the decision, and it had been made in the
third room of the Memory Palace, before

62
00:05:32,207 --> 00:05:38,658
he opened the door. Dead Chinese agents in a Hong Kong
hotel would be a diplomatic incident,

63
00:05:38,678 --> 00:05:44,636
and an incident was a story, and a story was
something he could not control. Nineteen

64
00:05:44,656 --> 00:05:50,755
unconscious Chinese agents in a Hong Kong hotel,
found by the real fire marshals twenty

65
00:05:50,775 --> 00:05:56,944
minutes later, zip-tied to a service railing with
their own restraints, with the syringe

66
00:05:56,964 --> 00:06:03,134
of horse sedative carefully capped and placed on the
chest of the woman in charge — that

67
00:06:03,154 --> 00:06:05,665
was not a story. That was a message.

68
00:06:07,235 --> 00:06:13,138
Hong Kong's police found them at two thirty-one.
The Ministry of State Security never

69
00:06:13,158 --> 00:06:19,269
acknowledged that they existed. Neither did the
Director. The hotel attributed the power

70
00:06:19,289 --> 00:06:25,331
failure to a faulty transformer, and apologised to
its guests, and gave everyone on the

71
00:06:25,351 --> 00:06:27,908
east tower a complimentary breakfast.

72
00:06:30,478 --> 00:06:36,655
At six in the morning, a note was slipped under the
door of his suite. It was handwritten,

73
00:06:36,675 --> 00:06:42,507
in beautiful English, on plain paper. It said: I
believe I owe you breakfast. It gave

74
00:06:42,527 --> 00:06:44,229
an address in Sheung Wan.

75
00:06:44,798 --> 00:06:45,894
He went alone.

76
00:06:46,463 --> 00:06:52,207
Wen Shulan was sitting at a table by the window of a
small tea house that had been there

77
00:06:52,227 --> 00:06:57,970
since before the British left, pouring tea from a
clay pot. She was fifty-nine. She wore

78
00:06:57,990 --> 00:07:03,537
a dark jacket and no jewellery. She did not stand
up when he came in, and she did not

79
00:07:03,557 --> 00:07:05,567
apologise for the night before.

80
00:07:06,257 --> 00:07:07,815
You did not kill them.

81
00:07:08,336 --> 00:07:08,956
No.

82
00:07:09,475 --> 00:07:10,903
May I ask why?

83
00:07:11,423 --> 00:07:16,467
Because you'd have had to avenge them. And I'd rather
you didn't have to do anything you

84
00:07:16,487 --> 00:07:17,331
didn't want to.

85
00:07:19,350 --> 00:07:25,988
She smiled, very slightly. It was the first smile
anyone at the Ministry of State Security

86
00:07:26,008 --> 00:07:28,725
had seen from her in seventeen years.

87
00:07:29,415 --> 00:07:31,857
That is either a kindness or a threat.

88
00:07:32,377 --> 00:07:36,108
In my experience, the most useful
things usually are both.

89
00:07:38,127 --> 00:07:44,004
They talked for two hours. Neither of them lied,
exactly. Neither of them told the truth,

90
00:07:44,024 --> 00:07:49,702
either. It was, the Director would reflect
afterwards, the most enjoyable conversation

91
00:07:49,722 --> 00:07:55,334
he had had in either of his lives — two people who
each knew precisely what the other

92
00:07:55,354 --> 00:08:01,032
was, and what the other wanted, and who had each
decided to enjoy the game rather than

93
00:08:01,052 --> 00:08:03,153
pretend it was not being played.

94
00:08:03,843 --> 00:08:08,950
I have listened to your speeches. All of them. I have
timed your silences. You speak like

95
00:08:08,970 --> 00:08:14,019
a man who learned his craft in front of crowds. Not
boardrooms. Crowds. Very large ones.

96
00:08:14,039 --> 00:08:14,825
From long ago.

97
00:08:15,345 --> 00:08:17,010
I watch a lot of old films.

98
00:08:17,531 --> 00:08:20,306
So do I. That is how I recognised it.

99
00:08:22,824 --> 00:08:26,990
In the quiet of one skull, the old voice
went very, very still.

100
00:08:27,560 --> 00:08:32,935
It was the first time in eight years that anyone in
this world had come within a mile

101
00:08:32,955 --> 00:08:38,329
of the truth. And it was a woman, in a tea house,
with a clay pot, who had done it by

102
00:08:38,349 --> 00:08:40,932
listening to the rhythm of his sentences.

103
00:08:43,121 --> 00:08:44,317
She knows.

104
00:08:44,837 --> 00:08:50,973
She suspects. It isn't the same thing. And she'll
never be able to say it out loud, because

105
00:08:50,993 --> 00:08:54,355
the moment she does, she sounds
like Owen Carrick.

106
00:08:56,375 --> 00:09:00,153
What do you want, Doctor Wesker? Not the
company. You. What do you want?

107
00:09:02,173 --> 00:09:07,875
He looked out of the window, at the old street waking
up — at the delivery bicycles, and

108
00:09:07,895 --> 00:09:12,620
the shutters going up, and the steam from a
noodle stall across the road.

109
00:09:13,311 --> 00:09:13,951
Order.

110
00:09:14,471 --> 00:09:15,688
For China?

111
00:09:16,208 --> 00:09:17,105
For everyone.

112
00:09:19,626 --> 00:09:25,668
Wen Shulan looked at him for a long time. Then she
set down her cup, very precisely, in

113
00:09:25,688 --> 00:09:27,828
the exact centre of its saucer.

114
00:09:28,518 --> 00:09:33,487
Then one day we will be enemies. Not today. I think
not for some years. But one day.

115
00:09:34,006 --> 00:09:34,690
I know.

116
00:09:35,211 --> 00:09:40,860
I would prefer, when that day comes, that it is you
and I at the table. Not men like Belov.

117
00:09:41,380 --> 00:09:42,213
So would I.

118
00:09:44,735 --> 00:09:50,210
They did not shake hands. She poured him a last cup
of tea, and he drank it, and then

119
00:09:50,230 --> 00:09:55,189
he left, and she sat alone at the table by the
window until the tea was cold.

120
00:09:57,259 --> 00:10:03,294
Three weeks later, Umbrella Life Sciences and Tianhe
Genomics announced a joint research

121
00:10:03,314 --> 00:10:09,143
venture in Singapore — a genomic medicine centre,
funded equally, governed by a board

122
00:10:09,163 --> 00:10:15,405
of four from each side. The financial press called it
a surprise. The American intelligence

123
00:10:15,425 --> 00:10:21,392
services called it, in a classified report, deeply
concerning. Martin Sloane called the

124
00:10:21,412 --> 00:10:24,695
Director, very angry, and was
told to trust him.

125
00:10:25,264 --> 00:10:30,677
It was not a surrender, and it was not an alliance.
It was two chess players agreeing

126
00:10:30,697 --> 00:10:36,238
to share a board for a while, because each of them
believed they would learn more about

127
00:10:36,258 --> 00:10:40,009
the other from across the table than
from across the world.

128
00:10:42,827 --> 00:10:46,115
Assessment. Rival recognised. Peer.

129
00:10:52,712 --> 00:10:57,024
Peer. The System had never used that
word before, about anyone.

130
00:10:57,595 --> 00:11:02,877
The Adolf half of the mind looked at it for a long
time, and did not like it at all.

131
00:11:03,566 --> 00:11:05,615
No one is our peer.

132
00:11:06,135 --> 00:11:08,932
She is. For now. That's why I
didn't kill her people.

133
00:11:09,451 --> 00:11:13,058
You are becoming sentimental
about your enemies.

134
00:11:13,577 --> 00:11:18,983
I'm becoming precise about them. You were never
precise about your enemies. You hated

135
00:11:19,003 --> 00:11:21,664
them all equally. It made you predictable.

136
00:11:23,684 --> 00:11:29,708
The old voice did not answer. But in the Memory
Palace, on the fourth floor, behind the

137
00:11:29,728 --> 00:11:35,682
door with no name, something wrote down the name Wen
Shulan, and underlined it, twice,

138
00:11:35,702 --> 00:11:40,615
the way Evelyn Marsh underlined things in
the margins of her notebooks.
